                       ENCODING CHANTS

  These additional notes are not necessary for an understanding of the chant data or for
  examining it and analysing it correctly. Some have been included to clarify fine points of
  the encoding or to identify points where, it has become clear, one procedure is to be
  preferred over another. In a few cases, procedures vary insignificantly from the printed
  descriptions.

  Most of the features described below were implemented as the chants were transcribed or
  encoded, rather than in a stage specially set aside for careful and systematic analysis. That
  kind of analysis remains to be done.
   In addition, such things as comments, exclamation points, and the symbol $ for
  sequential or borrowed motive, are often the result of applying traditional views about how
  plainsong works. It has become increasingly clear that this late medieval plainsong often
  calls into question the assumptions we have made about plainsong. The criteria for "normal"
  plainsong (whatever that is) seem less and less valid. For example, I have marked many
  places where the chant leaps a sixth (usually a major sixth), an interval usually said to be
  foreign to the balanced and restrained style of chant: at one stage I marked upward plicas,
  thinking them uncommon enough to note. Neither of these features now seems worth
  noting particularly. Perhaps these and other such notations in the encoding were the result
  of my own slim knowledge of the repertory, and of assumptions that were reasonable but
  that now seem unjustifiable.
   As a consequence, the appearance of some comments, the very placement of
  particular codes, and in particular the results derived from them, must be subjected, as with
  most of the data in the project, to careful research and consideration before conclusions can
  be drawn. I emphasized this point in the preface to LMLO: Sources and Chant.

  Sequential motives
  The symbol $ for sequential motives is placed inconsistently: sometimes it appears before a
  chantword that echoes an earlier one (this was clearly easier to implement); sometimes it
  appears after the earlier chantword; sometimes it appears in both places.
   Often the symbol appears in the middle of a chantword, when there are sequential
  motives within the chantword. Under "Indexing", I shall refer to a minor difficulty arising
  from the placement of the symbol in this position.

  Exclamations!
  The symbol ! used to draw attention to noteworthy points may, like the $ symbol, be used
  between or within chantwords. In the latter position it is subject to the difficulty just
  mentioned.

  The repertory
  One addition, found useful when finalizing the CHNTSRCH utility, consists in the
  addition to the data of a code that identifies the repertory or the style of the chant. It is
  easy to imagine wishing to know whether there are plainchant habits that apply only to the
  rhymed repertory, or to Dominican chant, for example. The repertory is thus identified,
  where necessary, between parentheses made unique by the letter z, otherwise not used in
  this context, thus (z repertory).
   In place of the word "repertory" are such codes as @r for rhymed, @p for prose,
  OP for Dominican, and the like. These codes can be specified after /xr in the command line
  of CHNTSRCH to limit matches (see the file CHNTSRCH.DOC).

  Enclitic -que
  As stated in the text, the enclitic -que is distinguished only in the text, using the + to
  separate the suffix. In chantwords this device was not feasible. In a few cases, however, the -que has been added in a separate line following the line of chantwords, using the truncation
  symbol #, thus:
   \ #que.3423<41= \()

  Mode letters (for verses and other items)
  The text states that in the verses of responsories, the mode is identified in the same way as
  for other genres. A somewhat more refined process has often been implemented.
        v
   It is sometimes clear that the verse uses the traditional responsory verse tone. In
  these cases, the letter v replaces the letter designating the Final. Thus in place of 1d the
  verse would have 1v.
        x or !
   Occasionally it seems that the verse tone underlies the tune used for the verse, but
  in an unusually elaborate or curious form. In these cases, the letter x may be appended to
  the usual code: 1dx or 1vx or 6cx
        p
   In some cases, the item is a dialogue or a verse that seems to use a psalm tone. Here
  the lettter may be a p: 1p

  Variant lines
  Occasionally variant lines have been allowed to produce the ancillary chant data: more
  often, the variants are so slight or short (e.g., single words) that the ancillary chant data
  does not make sense. The reports output by the program CHNTSRCH identify matches in
  such variant lines.

  Melodywords
  Melodywords are restricted to 10 pitches. Longer melodywords are truncated with the
  symbol for truncation, the hatch-mark #, thus: .12345#

  Incipits
  The formulation [-] used with for incipits in the chant encoding produces the (false) pitch -
  in the searchable chant: this is of negligible concern, since all the chant encodings if incipits
  are obviously incomplete.

  Truncations
  Chantwords longer than 30 characters have been truncated, the excess being isolated
  between #(# and ) as described in 826. These longwords in the chantword lines may appear
  in the melismalines delimited by \# and #() as two separate melismawords. Thus the word
  paradisi in CB29 has four syllables and 69 pitches. These are truncated for the indexing, and
  in the line of melismawords the chantword is presented twice, once as a word of one
  syllable and 60 pitches and a second time as a word of three syllables and 9 pitches.
   This result is clearly a defect that needs to be remedied. It occurs infrequently
  enough, enough, for its correction to be deferred.

  Indexing
  A minor but annoying problem has arisen in the process of indexing with WordCruncher.
  The options presented below depend on defining symbols in various ways in the control file
  used by WordCruncher and described in LMLO: Sources and Chants p. 210, Appendix 11.
  The definitions set out on that page may need to be modified, if certain of the options
  below are chosen.
   The difficulty arises with the symbols , (comma) $ and ! (and possibly other
  symbols) that may appear within chantwords.
   If these symbols are IGNORED by the indexing, then codes such as =$A2 for the
  second of a pair of antiphons in a commemoration, or ,v for virgin will appear in the index
  as =A2 and v respectively.
   If these symbols are indexed (probably as LOWERCASE), then these otherwise
  identical chantwords will appear separately in the index:
   chanttext.12,.34    and  chanttext.12.34
   another.354$.465.5  and  another.354.465.5
   oscit!.50.6         and  oscit.50.6
  Only in the last of these cases might the words appear widely separated in the index. Since
  usually such variants would be closeby in the index, it seems preferable to index the
  symbols.
   One alternative, circumventing this problem, is to replace the difficult (because
  ambiguous) symbols with characters not used for other purposes. This process can be
  accomplished relatively easily with some wordprocessors, but will require care and the use
  of symbols from the upper ASCII series.
   In any case, users must be careful to scan nearby entries in any index.

  Encoding: extra notes
  These notes are provided for those who might be interested in using the encoding for their
  own work with chant. Inevitably, as I have found at every stage of this project, users will
  discover circumstances for which an ad hoc solution will be necessary (such solutions are
  not very congenial to computerized processing!)
   In comments within the chantword lines, it is preferable to exclude all letters.
  Restricting the material between parentheses to pitches and symbols simplifies processing
  and some searches by wordprocessors, since letters will occur only in the chantwords
  themselves.

  Encoding: a concise guide
  Finally, I proved a concise guide to encoding chant.
   Chantword: textword with pitches and syllable separators. E.g., with pitches 1 and
  2: chantword.1.2
  Essential symbols
   Pitches, ascending: % * - 0 1 to 9 > where 1 is the Final. Other pitches: %! or >!
   Syllables: pitches separated by the dot .
   Flat: < for b-flat. The exclamation and < must be used for other pitches.
   Natural/sharp:  (ASCII 21)
   Repercussives: = (or ÷ for the quilisma)
  Inessential symbols
   Noteworthy point: !
   Sequential or borrowed motives: $
   Ligature separators and incises: ' (apostrophe)
   Clef: ^
   Plicas: , (comma)
  Articulations
   at the rhymedworD........;
   at the end of the intonation.....; or if also a rhymed word intonatioN.....;
   at the finalword....; or finalworD......;
   before the repetenda, may be rhymed or not: previousword.....; : Uppercase.first.letter
   at grammatical or syntactical punctuation: as above, perhaps with '' for an incise.

  All symbols are placed where they occur in the chantword, preceding the syllable separator
  if at that point, or are placed between words: word.1<23$!,.43'2 or word.1 ^! word.2
  
